<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime (NEWS-21, narrowed in NEWS-215).

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and Tauri enables it by default
  (`bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime`, default true). That matters more here than in
  a typical Tauri app, because the sidecar is a real Node binary: V8 compiles
  JavaScript at runtime, and the hardened runtime blocks exactly that unless the
  entitlements below say otherwise. Without them the app signs and notarizes
  cleanly and then dies on launch — on someone else's machine, not the build
  machine, which is the worst possible place to find out.

  **This set now matches ~/Documents/glassbox's exactly.** Glassbox ships the same
  Node-sidecar architecture and has notarized and shipped with these three for
  months, which is better evidence than deriving the set from Node's own binary:
  what matters is what the notary service accepts and what a Node sidecar needs
  at launch, and glassbox is a live answer to both.

  Deliberately absent, and why:

  - `com.apple.security.get-task-allow` — permits a debugger to attach, and the
    notary service **rejects** any binary carrying it. Node ships it because
    Node's binaries are signed but not notarized; ours are both.
  - `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables` — nothing sets
    `DYLD_*` for the sidecar, and it is an injection surface. An entitlement that
    isn't needed shouldn't be granted.
  - `com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection` — this **was**
    here, derived from Node's own binary, on the reasoning that V8 writes and
    executes the same pages. Removed in NEWS-215: it is the broadest of the set,
    Apple discourages it, and glassbox runs a Node sidecar without it. If a
    signed build's sidecar ever fails to start, this is the first thing to put
    back — but verify rather than assume, because a missing `allow-jit` produces
    the same symptom.

  The risk shape here is unusual and worth restating: **an entitlement change
  cannot be validated by a green build.** Signing, notarization and stapling all
  succeed regardless; the failure appears only when the packaged app is launched
  on a Mac. See docs/manual-test-plan.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!-- V8 writes and executes JIT-compiled code. Without this the sidecar
         cannot run any JavaScript at all under the hardened runtime. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- V8 also maps executable pages it did not sign. `allow-jit` alone covers
         the MAP_JIT path; this covers the rest, and Node ships both. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Lets the process load libraries not signed by the same team. The staged
         tree has no native addons today (`find src-tauri/server -name '*.node'`
         is empty), so this is not strictly required — it is here to match
         glassbox, which is the configuration with real notarization mileage. It
         is also the entitlement whose absence would break the first native
         dependency anyone adds, with a failure that looks like a mysterious
         dlopen error. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
